{I'd gone up to Teesside for a week's chess "holiday" and didn't expect to be playing a strong IM in the first round! It actually worked out well. We both won all our remaining games and came equal first.} 1. c4 Nf6 2. Nc3 e6 3. d4 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Nge2 d5 6. a3 Be7 7. cxd5 exd5 8. g3 c6 9. Bg2 Bf5 10. O-O Bd6 11. f3 Re8 12. e4 dxe4 13. fxe4 Bxe4 14. Rxf6 Bxg2 15. Rxd6 Qxd6 16. Kxg2 {The stem game on the database is Evans,L-Maccioni Seisdedos,A/Dubrovnik ol (Men) 1950 (15)/1-0. My 1965 (10th edition) copy of Modern Chess Openings (co-edited by Larry Evans) had this line as giving White a good advantage, whereas interestingly Houdini thinks it's better for Black! (MCO 10, page 392 - JS)} Nd7 17. Bf4 ({Rybka and Houdini prefer} 17. Qb3 c5 18. d5 {which seems to me the wrong plan entirely.}) 17... Qe6 18. Qd3 Nb6 19. Rf1 Nd5 20. Be5 $6 (20. Bc1 Rad8 21. Nf4 $14) (20. Rf2 Rad8 21. Nxd5 Qxd5+ 22. Qf3 {Kluger,G-Florian,T/HUN-ch 12th Budapest 1957/1-0 and white went on to win in}) 20... f6 ({Rybka thinks that} 20... Rad8 {is stronger}) 21. Nf4 $2 (21. Bf4 $142 Nb6 22. Rf2 $14) 21... Qd7 22. Nfxd5 fxe5 ({I had thought only of} 22... cxd5 23. Bf4) 23. Ne3 (23. Nf6+ $1 {may be best} gxf6 24. Ne4 Rf8 (24... Kg7 25. Nxf6 e4 26. Nxe8+ $11) 25. Nxf6+ Rxf6 26. Rxf6 Qd5+ 27. Kg1 Qxd4+ 28. Qxd4 exd4 29. Rd6 {reaches a drawn ending}) 23... exd4 24. Nf5 Rad8 25. Ne4 Qe6 (25... Qd5 $142 $5 26. Rf4 Kh8) 26. Rf4 c5 $6 (26... b6 $142 27. Kg1 $11) 27. Nxc5 Qe2+ 28. Rf2 Qxd3 29. Nxd3 g6 ({If} 29... Re3 30. Nf4 Re4 31. Rd2 $14) 30. Nh6+ Kg7 31. Ng4 h5 (31... Re7 32. Nge5 $14) 32. Nge5 Rd5 $2 (32... Re7 $14) 33. Rf7+ $4 (33. Nxg6 $1 Rd6 (33... Rb5 34. Nh4 Re3 35. Nf4 $18 Reb3 36. Ne6+ Kh6 37. Nxd4 Rxb2 38. Nxb5 Rxb5 39. Rf5) 34. Nge5 Re7 35. Rf5 Rh6 36. h4 $18 {However, I was merely intent on getting to a drawn rook and pawn ending. I only noticed this tactic years later inputting to the computer!}) 33... Kg8 34. Rxb7 Rexe5 35. Nxe5 Rxe5 36. Rxa7 Re2+ 37. Kf3 Rxb2 38. Rd7 Rxh2 39. Rxd4 Ra2 40. Ra4 Kg7 41. Ra6 Kh6 42. a4 Ra1 43. Ra8 Ra3+ 44. Kg2 Kh7 45. Ra7+ Kg8 (45... Kh6 46. Ra8 $11) 46. Kf2 {and we agreed a draw} 1/2-1/2

